= Principal–agent problem =

== Overview ==
'''Principal–agent problem''' arises when one party delegates action to another party whose interests, information, or risk exposure differ in ways that make desired behavior difficult to observe or induce.

The principal wants the agent to act on the principal's behalf, but the principal may not observe the agent's effort, information, or private type. Contracts, monitoring, incentives, ownership, reputation, and allocation of decision rights are common responses.

Moral hazard concerns hidden actions or effort after an agreement. Adverse selection concerns hidden information or types before or during contracting. These are related information problems but should not be collapsed.

The principal–agent framework applies beyond employment: shareholders and managers, clients and professionals, citizens and officials, insurers and insured parties, platform operators and participants, and many other delegated relationships can have principal–agent structure.

== Boundaries ==
* principal–agent problem ≠ employer–employee relationship only
* moral hazard ≠ adverse selection
* delegation ≠ guaranteed incentive alignment

